  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 431 AM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nCurrent surface analysis places low over British Columbia into\nwestern Alberta, while high settles through the Dakotas. Upper\nlevel analysis places low over the Arizona/Utah border with\nsplit flow over the western CONUS. We remain near the confluence\nof the flow, with west to northwest flow over our area. Speaking\nof our area, low clouds remain rather entrenched generally along\nand east of the Missouri River, which have kept temperatures on\nthe warmer side, whereas areas that have cleared have dipped\ninto the mid 20s to around 30. With the clouds reluctant to\ndepart, have decided to cancel the Freeze Warning over our \neastern areas. \n\nFor today, aforementioned clouds over eastern parts of our area\nwill gradually dissipated and move out as the day moves on. \nWhere it has cleared, areas of fog will remain a concern through\nmid-morning. \n\nLater today into tonight winds will shift southerly as\naforementioned surface low shifts into Saskatchewan. Upper low\nstarts to lift northeast and open, bringing rain chances mainly\nto the south of us, though can't rule out a few showers sneaking\ninto our southern areas this evening. Later tonight, low moves \nover Manitoba as a cold front starts its push through our area, \nshifting increasing winds northwesterly.\n\nOn Thursday, cold front will continue its progression through\nour area in the morning, with winds increasing in its wake.\nLatest rounds of forecast soundings continue to support winds\ngusting to 45 mph. Can't rule out a stray shower with this\nsystem, mainly around the Turtle Mountains as the upper wave\nwith this system drops southerly. \n\nA cold night is then on tap for Thursday night as a surface \nhigh settles into the Northern Plains and cooler air continues \nto slide in. Lows in the 20s are possible, especially over \nwestern areas. \n\nCooler air does not reside over our area long an an upper ridge\nto the west builds towards our area. By Saturday, highs in the\n70s are expected over various parts of our area. The start of \nthe upcoming week is expected to remain dry with seasonably mild\ntemperatures.",
